    Based on the search results, here are the key findings regarding prime counting function π(x) algorithms and their computational complexity:
    
    ## Recent Research (2025-2026)
    
    Using existing results on the complexity of evaluating the prime-counting function π(x), a binary search approach computes p_n in O(√n(log n)⁴) time. Assuming the Riemann Hypothesis and Cramér's conjecture, researchers have constructed a tighter interval allowing computation in O(√n(log^(7/2)n)log log n) time, which while conditional, suggests that further refinements to prime gap estimates may yield provably faster methods for computing primes.
    
    ## Existing Algorithms and Complexity
    
    Several established algorithms have different time complexities:
    
    - The Sieve of Eratosthenes has a time complexity of O(x log log x) and generally requires O(x) space.
    
    - The 1985 Lagarias-Miller-Odlyzko algorithm based on Meissel's formula has a lower runtime complexity of O(x^(2/3)/log x) operations.
    
    - The Lagarias-Odlyzko method using Perron's formula resulted in an O(x^(1/2+ε))-algorithm (both for memory and time) to calculate π(x), which is asymptotically the fastest method available today.
    
    - Modern implementations achieve a computational complexity of O(n^(3/4)/((log n)²)), allowing calculation of primes to 1e11 in about 22 milliseconds.
    
