    ## Analytic Prime Counting Algorithms
    
    Formulas for prime-counting functions come in two kinds: arithmetic and analytic. Analytic formulas for prime-counting were the first used to prove the prime number theorem, stemming from the work of Riemann and von Mangoldt, and are generally known as explicit formulae.
    
    ## Connection to Zeta Zeros
    
    The zeros of the Riemann zeta function in the critical strip (where the real part is between zero and one) are central to these formulas. The sum over non-trivial zeta zeros in the formula for the prime-counting function describes the fluctuations of the function while the remaining terms give the "smooth" part of the prime-counting function.
    
    ## Computational Methods
    
    The Lagarias–Odlyzko method remains the (asymptotically) fastest known π(x) counting method, requiring only O(x^(1/2+ε)) bit complexity and O(x^(1/4+ε)) memory. Additionally, the Lagarias-Odlyzko algorithm uses analytic continuation and the properties of the zeta function to compute π(x), and is more efficient than the Meissel-Lehmer algorithm for very large x.
    
