    23	## Proposal 25 — Liouville-Parity Triangulation
    24	
    25	### Idea
    26	Instead of computing π(x) exactly, build a *parity oracle* P₂(x) = π(x) mod 2 and locate primes via parity flips. The classical identity
    27	$$ L(x) = \sum_{n\le x} \lambda(n) = -\pi(x) + \pi(x^{1/2}) - \pi(x^{1/3}) + \cdots
    28	   + (\text{contributions from }\Omega(n)\ge 2) $$
    29	yields, mod 2, a closed-form recursion that ties π(x) parity to L(x) parity and the squarefree count Q(x) = (6/π²)x + O(√x). Because both Q(x) and the (squarefree, Ω≥2) tail are arithmetic and tractable, the bottleneck is L(x) mod 2.
