    Based on the search results, here's what I found about the Guth-Maynard work on Riemann zeta zeros:
    
    ## Guth and Maynard's Breakthrough
    
    Guth and Maynard made the first substantial improvement to a classical 1940 bound of Ingham regarding the zeroes of the Riemann zeta function (and more generally, controlling the large values of various Dirichlet series).
    
    The authors prove new bounds for how often Dirichlet polynomials can take large values, giving improved estimates for a Dirichlet polynomial of length N taking values of size close to N^{3/4}, which is the critical situation for several estimates in analytic number theory connected to prime numbers and the Riemann zeta function.
    
    ## Key Results
    
    As a consequence, they deduce a zero density estimate N(σ,T)≤ T^{30(1-σ)/13+o(1)} and asymptotics for primes in short intervals of length x^{17/30+o(1)}.
    
    Maynard and Guth have succeeded in significantly improving Ingham's estimate for the first time, with the zeta function in the range 0.75 ≤ x ≤ 1 having at most y^{13/25+c} zeros with an imaginary part of at most y.
    
    ## Technical Approach
    
    For this advance, Maynard and Guth initially used well-known methods from Fourier analysis, similar to techniques used to break down sound into its overtones, but then performed a number of clever and unexpected maneuvers.
    
    ## Related Work on Hiary's Algorithm
    
    While your search included "Hiary algorithm," the Guth-Maynard paper focuses on zero-density estimates rather than directly using Hiary's computational algorithms. Hiary developed algorithms that reduce the complexity of evaluating ζ(1/2 + it) at a single point to O(t^{4/13+o(1)}), compared to the standard Riemann-Siegel formula's complexity of O(t^{1/2}), with a simpler version achieving O(t^{1/3+o(1)}).
    
    
