    ## Main Paper
    
    The foundational work shows that integer partitions detect prime numbers in an unexpected way, answering a question of Schneider by demonstrating that primes are the solutions to special equations in partition functions. This paper was authored by William Craig, Jan-Willem van Ittersum, and Ken Ono.
    
    The paper provides an example where an integer n≥2 is prime if and only if a specific equation in MacMahon's partition functions equals zero. More generally, the authors prove that for "MacMahonesque" partition functions, there are infinitely many such prime detecting equations with constant coefficients.
    
    ## Follow-up Research
    
    Building on the results of Craig, van Ittersum, and Ono, subsequent work provides a refined understanding of MacMahon's partition functions and their variants, including their quasi-modular properties and new prime-detecting expressions.
    
    Additional research by Kevin Gomez shows how to construct explicit expressions in partition functions of MacMahon that detect prime numbers, and demonstrates how to generalize these constructions to detect infinitely many such expressions in "MacMahonesque" functions.
    
    The original paper on arXiv (2405.06451) was posted in May 2024 and was later published in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences.
    
    
