    ## Recent Work on Hilbert-Pólya Operator and Riemann Hypothesis
    
    A recent paper by Enderalp Yakaboylu introduces a non-symmetric operator R on L²([0,∞)) whose spectrum is related to zeros of the Riemann zeta function, and constructs the compression of R to the spectral subspace associated with nontrivial Riemann zeros, showing it is intertwined with its adjoint by a positive semidefinite operator W.
    
    The positivity of W, viewed as an operator-theoretic form of Weil's positivity criterion, enforces that all nontrivial Riemann zeros lie on the critical line, in accordance with the Riemann Hypothesis.
    
    The paper demonstrates the existence of a self-adjoint Hamiltonian whose eigenvalues correspond to the simple nontrivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function, with all such zeros lying on the critical line, marking a significant step toward a proof of the Riemann Hypothesis.
    
    The work also builds on earlier contributions: In 1998, Alain Connes formulated a trace formula that is actually equivalent to the Riemann hypothesis.
    
    The Hilbert-Pólya Conjecture offers a potential approach to proving the RH through spectral theory and creates a bridge between number theory and quantum physics.
    
    The paper appears to have been significantly updated through March 2026, with the version history showing continuous refinements to the approach.
    
    
