    Based on the search results, here are the main findings related to zero density estimates for the Riemann zeta function:
    
    ## Recent Improvements (2024-2026)
    
    The most recent work provides the first explicit log-free zero-density estimate for ζ(s) of the form N(σ,T)≤AT^B(1−σ). This estimate becomes the sharpest known explicit zero-density estimate uniformly for σ∈[α₀,1], with 0.985≤α₀≤0.992 and 3·10¹²<T≤exp(6.7·10¹²).
    
    ## Earlier Results and Methods
    
    In 1937, Ingham showed the asymptotic result N(σ,T)=O(T^(8/3(1−σ))(log T)⁵). Ramaré recently proved an explicit version of this estimate, with a generalization of the method used yielding an explicit bound of similar shape while improving the constants.
    
    Another approach introduced a new method to detect the zeros of the Riemann zeta function which is sensitive to the vertical distribution of zeros, allowing improvements to the Ingham-Huxley zero-density estimate.
    
    These improvements represent significant progress in obtaining explicit, computable bounds on the distribution of zeros of the Riemann zeta function in the critical strip.
    
    
