    **3. PRIMES in TC^0, BPSW, AKS variants** — Only one tangential hit: a 2026-04-07 blog post by Jeremy Kun on deterministic Miller-Rabin for limited bit width (https://www.jeremykun.com/2026/04/07/deterministic-miller-rabin/). Engineering note, not a complexity-class result. **Verdict: no change.**
    
    **4. New π(x) algorithms (Deléglise-Rivat / Gourdon / primecount)** — primecount remains at v7.19 (2025); no 2026 release. No new arXiv preprint on combinatorial π(x) in window. The October 2025 Aggarwal note (arXiv:2510.16285) reaffirms O(√n · log^4 n) for nth prime via π(x) binary search; nothing newer. **Verdict: no change.**
    
    **5. Riemann zeta zeros / Hiary / Guth-Maynard follow-ups** — Yakaboylu's "On the Existence of the Hilbert-Pólya Hamiltonian" (arXiv:2408.15135) was updated to v10 with notes through March 12, 2026 — possibly within window but unclear if revision falls in 4/5–4/25. Claims a self-adjoint operator with simple-zero spectrum; community has not validated. No Guth-Maynard follow-up in the window. **Verdict: unclear (Yakaboylu); no change to algorithmic barrier even if accepted.**
    
    **6. Hardy-Littlewood / Selberg / Helfgott-Thompson sieves** — arXiv:2602.22720 (Feb 2026) on "Explicit Result for the Sum of Two Almost Primes" uses Selberg sieve, but is not algorithmic speedup. Nothing in 4/5–4/25. **Verdict: no change.**
    
    **7. Sub-x^{1/2} algorithms for π(x) or p_n** — None claimed. Aggarwal's October 2025 result remains current ceiling. **Verdict: no change.**
    
    **8. Brandt diagonalization MKtP follow-ups** — No new follow-ups in window. Original Brandt (eprint 2024/687) still standalone. **Verdict: no change.**
    
    **9. Ono / van Ittersum partition prime detection extensions** — arXiv:2601.21267 (Jan 2026, "On the structure of prime-detecting quasimodular forms in higher levels") extends framework to higher levels; arXiv:2412.19180 (Gomez) covers MacMahonesque variants. Both predate the window. No April 2026 additions detected. **Verdict: no change to algorithmic barrier — these remain non-constructive prime-detection identities, not faster computation.**
    
    **10. Connes / Yakaboylu Hilbert-Pólya** — Connes posted "The Riemann Hypothesis: Past, Present and a Letter Through Time" (arXiv:2602.04022, Feb 2026) — survey/perspective piece, not new operator. Yakaboylu activity noted in (5). **Verdict: no algorithmic implications.**
    
    ## Bottom Line
    **No paper in the 2026-04-05 → 2026-04-25 window changes the asymptotic barrier for computing p(n).** The Yakaboylu Hamiltonian construction continues to be revised but, even if accepted, would prove RH without yielding a faster π(x) or p(n) algorithm. The October 2025 Aggarwal note remains the most recent direct statement on nth-prime complexity. Recommend updating `literature/state_of_art_2026.md` only with the Connes survey (2602.04022) and Yakaboylu v10 status note; no action needed on `OPEN_PROBLEMS.md` or `CLOSED_PATHS.md`.
    
