    5	## What Was Tested
    6	Three approaches: (1) linear complexity of the prime indicator sequence over GF(2) via Berlekamp-Massey, (2) Mills' constant computability, (3) Matiyasevich-Robinson polynomial for prime generation.
    7	
    8	## Key Findings
    9	- Berlekamp-Massey on prime indicator over GF(2): linear complexity = N/2 for all tested sequence lengths -- maximal, confirming the sequence is pseudorandom over GF(2)
    10	- This matches Session 24 results: L/N = 0.5 for all finite fields tested
    11	- Mills' constant: circular -- computing A requires knowing all primes; no independent characterization exists
    12	- Matiyasevich-Robinson polynomial: the 26-variable polynomial exists but finding the "right" inputs for the nth prime requires search over exponentially many candidates
    13	- No shortcut to evaluate the polynomial at inputs producing p(n) without already knowing p(n)
    14	
    15	## Verdict
    16	**CLOSED**
    17	**Failure Mode:** Circularity (C) for Mills and Matiyasevich; Information Loss (I) for LFSR -- linear complexity N/2 means the sequence is incompressible over GF(2).
    18	
    19	## One-Line Summary
    20	LFSR prime encoding: linear complexity = N/2 (maximal); Mills' constant circular; Matiyasevich polynomial requires exponential search.
