    ## Overview
    
    The prime counting function is the function π(x) giving the number of primes less than or equal to a given number x.
    
    ## Historical Algorithm Development
    
    Several major algorithms have been developed for computing π(x) with different time complexities:
    
    - The sieve of Eratosthenes, known until the early 19th century as the most efficient method for counting primes, has a running time of O(x log log x) operations.
    
    - Legendre's formula (1830) uses the inclusion-exclusion principle to calculate the number of primes below x without enumerating the individual primes, with a running time of O(∛x).
    
    - In 1959, D. H. Lehmer extended Meissel's formula and slightly improved the running time to O(x^(3/8)) space.
    
    - In 1985, J. C. Lagarias, V. S. Miller and A. M. Odlyzko published a new algorithm based on Meissel's formula which has a lower runtime complexity of O(x^(2/3)/log x) operations.
    
    ## Recent Research (2025)
    
    Using existing results on the complexity of evaluating the prime-counting function π(x), the binary search approach computes the nth prime in O(√n (log n)⁴) time. This represents recent work that builds on established complexity results for computing π(x).
    
    Note that the search results do not contain specific information about polylogarithmic complexity algorithms for π(x) from 2025.
    
    
